{"cuisine/yup-ik-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/yup-ik-cuisine","n":"Yup'ik cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Alaska Native foodways","cuisine/alaska-native-foodways"]],"sub":"Indigenous foodways, Yukon-Kuskokwim Delta and Bristol Bay, Alaska","names":"Yup'ik, Cup'ik","intro":[["Yup'ik and Cup'ik people live along Alaska's southwest coast and up the Yukon and Kuskokwim rivers. Summer is fish camp, when ",["Salmon","ingredient/salmon"]," is cut, dried and smoked to last the winter, and fall is for berries and greens from the tundra."],[["Akutaq","dish/akutaq"],", from a Yup'ik word for \"mixed together,\" is whipped fat with berries and sometimes fish. Dried fish dipped in ",["Seal oil","ingredient/seal-oil"]," is a favorite at any time of year."]],"src":"note","sources":[{"name":"Alaska Native Heritage Center","url":"https://www.alaskanative.net/"},{"name":"University of Alaska Museum of the North, Alaska Native cultures guide","url":"https://uaf.edu/museum/education/docents/pdfs/NC2-3_Intro_2014.pdf"}],"actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"yup-ik-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yup'ik_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":1,"links":[["Akutaq","dish/akutaq"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Alaska Native foodways","cuisine/alaska-native-foodways"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Animal fats and oils","ingredient/animal-fats-and-oils"],["Seal oil","ingredient/seal-oil"],["Berry","ingredient/berry"],["Whitefish","ingredient/whitefish"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Alaska Native foodways","cuisine/alaska-native-foodways"]]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/9/93/Smoked_Salmon.jpg","w":500,"h":484,"by":"GEMalone","lic":"CC BY 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Smoked_Salmon.jpg"}},"cuisine/mordovian-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/mordovian-cuisine","n":"Mordovian cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Russia","intro":[["Mordovian cuisine is the traditional cuisine of the Mordovians, who now live in Mordovia and surrounding areas. It consists of a variety of dishes, based on geographical, cultural and climate features of the region, with fish traditionally featured heavily."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"mordovian-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mordovian_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/9/9a/Erzya_kaymak_%28panzhakay%29_05.jpg/500px-Erzya_kaymak_%28panzhakay%29_05.jpg","w":500,"h":281,"by":"Vaija","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Erzya_kaymak_(panzhakay)_05.jpg"}},"cuisine/danish-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/danish-cuisine","n":"Danish cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Denmark","intro":[["Danish cuisine originated from the peasant population's own local produce and was enhanced by cooking techniques developed in the late 19th century and the wider availability of goods during and after the Industrial Revolution. Open sandwiches, known as smørrebrød, which in their basic form are the usual fare for lunch, can be considered a national speciality when prepared and garnished with a variety of ingredients. Hot meals are typically prepared with meat or fish. Substantial meat and fish dishes includes flæskesteg (roast pork with crackling) and kogt torsk (poached cod) with mustard sauce and trimmings. Ground meats (pork, veal or beef) became widespread during the industrial revolution and traditional dishes that are still popular include frikadeller (meat balls), karbonader (breaded pork patties) and medisterpølse (fried sausage). Denmark is known for its Carlsberg and Tuborg beers and for its akvavit and bitters, but amongst the Danes themselves imported wine has gained steadily in popularity since the 1960s."],["Cooking in Denmark has always been inspired by foreign and continental practises and the use of imported tropical spices like cinnamon, cardamom, nutmeg and black pepper can be traced to the Danish cuisine of the Middle Ages and some even to the Vikings."],["Since the early 2000s, some Danish chefs have developed the new Danish cuisine, an way of cooking based on high-quality local produce. This new philosophy and cuisine has attracted the attention of, and been celebrated by, the international gourmet community. It has contributed with a considerable number of highly acclaimed restaurants in Copenhagen and the province, with some of them awarded Michelin stars."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"danish-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Danish_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Dishes","count":10,"links":[["Æbleflæsk","dish/blefl-sk"],["Æbleskiver","dish/bleskiver"],["Danish pastry","dish/danish-pastry"],["Glögg","dish/glogg"],["Kransekage","dish/kransekage"],["Kringle","dish/kringle"],["Labskaus","dish/labskaus"],["Risalamande","dish/risalamande"],["Rødgrød","dish/r-dgr-d"],["Smørrebrød","dish/sm-rrebr-d"]]},{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Cook Yourself Happy: The Danish Way","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Caroline Fleming","note":"Danish home cooking and family recipes from a Danish author.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781911127284","suggested":true,"isbn":"9781911127284"}]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"],["Almond","ingredient/almond"],["Cream","ingredient/cream"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Butter","ingredient/butter"],["Apple","ingredient/apple"],["Bacon","ingredient/bacon"],["Cinnamon","ingredient/cinnamon"],["Peppercorn","ingredient/peppercorn"],["Yellow onion","ingredient/yellow-onion"],["Buttermilk","ingredient/buttermilk"],["Yeast","ingredient/yeast"],["Mulled wine","ingredient/mulled-wine"],["Raisin","ingredient/raisin"],["Wine","ingredient/wine"],["Egg white","ingredient/egg-white"],["Prunus dulcis","ingredient/prunus-dulcis"],["Rye flour","ingredient/rye-flour"],["Corned beef","ingredient/corned-beef"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Potatoes","ingredient/potatoes"],["Salted meat","ingredient/salted-meat"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Within Danish cooking","count":2,"links":[["Faroese cuisine","cuisine/faroese-cuisine"],["New Nordic cuisine","cuisine/new-nordic-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"European cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/3/3e/Flickr_-_cyclonebill_-_Frikadeller_med_ristet_rugbr%C3%B8d_og_cornichons.jpg/500px-Flickr_-_cyclonebill_-_Frikadeller_med_ristet_rugbr%C3%B8d_og_cornichons.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"cyclonebill","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flickr_-_cyclonebill_-_Frikadeller_med_ristet_rugbr%C3%B8d_og_cornichons.jpg"}},"cuisine/mennonite-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/mennonite-cuisine","n":"Mennonite cuisine","c":"#a99fb5","crumb":[["Christian diet","cuisine/christian-dietary-laws"]],"sub":"Religious cuisine or diet","intro":[["Mennonite cuisine is food associated with Mennonites, members of Christian denominations that came out of the Anabaptist movement in sixteenth-century Switzerland and Netherlands. Because of persecution and discrimination against their close-knit communities, they migrated to Prussia, Russia, Poland and the Americas. Groups like the Russian Mennonites developed a sense of ethnicity, which included cuisine adapted from the countries where they lived. Thus, the term Mennonite cuisine does not apply to all, or even most, Mennonites today, especially those outside of the traditional ethnic Mennonite groups. Nor is the food necessarily unique to Mennonites, most of the dishes being variations on recipes common to the countries (Netherlands, Poland, Ukraine, Russia, Latin America) where they reside or resided in the past."],["Mennonites do not have any customary dietary restrictions as exist in some other religious groups. Some conservative Mennonites abstain from alcohol, but other Mennonites do not, with Mennonite distilleries existing as early as the late 16th century."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mennonite_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Christian diet","cuisine/christian-dietary-laws"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"More-with-Less Cookbook","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Doris Janzen Longacre, 1976","note":"Herald Press cookbook of recipes gathered from Mennonite cooks on eating simply and frugally; a 40th anniversary edition followed in 2016.","url":"","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Christian diet","cuisine/christian-dietary-laws"]]}],"cont":null,"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/27/Farmersausage.jpg/500px-Farmersausage.jpg","w":500,"h":292,"by":"Mennowiki","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Farmersausage.jpg"}},"dish/garbure":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/garbure","n":"Garbure","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 2 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Garbure is a savoury French dish, described either as a soup or a stew.  It is a one-pot dish of vegetables – mainly root vegetables – with one or more of several meats, slowly cooked in an earthenware dish in an oven. The cooking liquid is sometimes served as soup before the meat and vegetables are served as a main course. The food writer Curnonsky named garbure as one of the four great regional dishes of France."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-garbure"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garbure"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["French cuisine","cuisine/french-cuisine"],["New Caledonian cuisine","cuisine/new-caledonian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":1,"links":[["Ham","ingredient/ham"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Pardon Your French","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"pardonyourfrench.com","note":"Audrey Le Goff, a French cook, shares classic and regional French home recipes.","url":"https://pardonyourfrench.com","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"La Cuisine de Géraldine","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"lacuisinedegeraldine.fr","note":"French home cooking blog by Géraldine Leverd, also author of The New French Kitchen.","url":"https://lacuisinedegeraldine.fr/en/about-us","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"New Caledonia Tourism: Kanak cooking","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"newcaledonia.travel","note":"Official tourism board overview of Kanak food, including bougna cooked in an earth oven.","url":"https://www.newcaledonia.travel/nz/gastronomy-wine/gastronomy/kanak-cooking","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"More from French cuisine","links":[["Albufera sauce","dish/albufera-sauce"],["Andouillette","dish/andouillette"],["Aspic","dish/aspic"],["Bayonne ham","dish/bayonne-ham"],["Beef Stroganoff","dish/beef-stroganoff"],["Beurre maître d'hôtel","dish/beurre-maitre-d-hotel"],["Beurre manié","dish/beurre-manie"],["Bisque (food)","dish/bisque-food"],["Blanquette de veau","dish/blanquette-de-veau"],["Blini","dish/blini"],["Bolinhos de bacalhau","dish/bolinhos-de-bacalhau"],["Bouillabaisse","dish/bouillabaisse"],["Bourride","dish/bourride"],["Brioche","dish/brioche"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Calisson","dish/calisson"],["Canapé","dish/canape"],["Cervelle de canut","dish/cervelle-de-canut"],["Cream of sorrel soup","dish/cream-of-sorrel-soup"],["Crêpes Suzette","dish/crepes-suzette"],["Croquembouche","dish/croquembouche"],["Crozets de Savoie","dish/crozets-de-savoie"],["Crudités","dish/crudites"],["Damien Pignolet","dish/damien-pignolet"],["Daube","dish/daube"],["Demi-glace","dish/demi-glace"],["Duxelles","dish/duxelles"],["Espagnole sauce","dish/espagnole-sauce"],["Fin gras du Mézenc","dish/fin-gras-du-mezenc"],["Flammekueche","dish/flammekueche"],["Foie gras","dish/foie-gras"],["Foin de Crau","dish/foin-de-crau"],["Fraisier","dish/fraisier"],["French onion soup","dish/french-onion-soup"],["French restaurant","dish/french-restaurant"],["Fricassee","dish/fricassee"],["Gâteau Basque","dish/gateau-basque"],["Gugelhupf","dish/gugelhupf"],["Hollandaise sauce","dish/hollandaise-sauce"],["Kig ha farz","dish/kig-ha-farz"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/f/fb/Garbure_%281%29.jpg/500px-Garbure_%281%29.jpg","w":500,"h":346,"by":"Arnold Gatilao from Fremont, CA, USA","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Garbure_(1).jpg"}},"dish/gulai":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/gulai","n":"Gulai","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 4 cuisines","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Gulai is a type of spiced stew commonly found in the culinary traditions of Indonesia and other parts of Maritime Southeast Asia, including Brunei, Singapore, Malaysia, and southern Thailand. Closely associated with both Malay and Minangkabau cuisines, it is characterised by a rich, aromatic sauce made from coconut milk and a blend of ground spices, typically including turmeric, coriander, chilli and other local aromatics. Gulai is usually prepared with meat, fish, offal or vegetables and is typically served with rice. In English, it is sometimes described as Malay curry or Indonesian curry."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-gulai"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gulai"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine"],["Padang food","cuisine/padang-food"],["Singaporean cuisine","cuisine/singaporean-cuisine"],["Singaporean Chinese cuisine","cuisine/singaporean-cuisine-chinese-inspired"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":15,"links":[["Bird","ingredient/bird"],["Black pepper","ingredient/black-pepper"],["Bumbu (seasoning)","ingredient/bumbu-seasoning"],["Caraway","ingredient/caraway"],["Chilli pepper","ingredient/chilli-pepper"],["Cinnamon","ingredient/cinnamon"],["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Coriander","ingredient/coriander"],["Fennel","ingredient/fennel"],["Galangal","ingredient/galangal"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Offal","ingredient/offal"],["Shallot","ingredient/shallot"],["Turmeric","ingredient/turmeric"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Daily Cooking Quest","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"dailycookingquest.com","note":"Indonesian home cook Anita's blog with a large archive of Indonesian recipes.","url":"https://dailycookingquest.com/cuisines/indonesian.html","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"My Singapore Food","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"mysingaporefood.com","note":"Recipe archive focused on documenting Singaporean heritage dishes.","url":"https://mysingaporefood.com/","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"More from Indonesian cuisine","links":[["Arem-arem","dish/arem-arem"],["Arsik","dish/arsik"],["Asam pedas","dish/asam-pedas"],["Ayam goreng","dish/ayam-goreng"],["Ayam goreng kalasan","dish/ayam-goreng-kalasan"],["Ayam panggang","dish/ayam-panggang"],["Ayam Taliwang","dish/ayam-taliwang"],["Bajigur","dish/bajigur"],["Bakakak hayam","dish/bakakak-hayam"],["Bakpia","dish/bakpia"],["Bakwan","dish/bakwan"],["Bekasang","dish/bekasang"],["Betutu","dish/betutu"],["Bika ambon","dish/bika-ambon"],["Binte biluhuta","dish/binte-biluhuta"],["Bir pletok","dish/bir-pletok"],["Bolu kukus","dish/bolu-kukus"],["Botok","dish/botok"],["Brem","dish/brem"],["Bubur kacang hijau","dish/bubur-kacang-hijau"],["Bubur ketan hitam","dish/bubur-ketan-hitam"],["Bubur pedas","dish/bubur-pedas"],["Buntil","dish/buntil"],["Burgo (food)","dish/burgo-food"],["Cendol","dish/cendol"],["Cenil","dish/cenil"],["Cilok","dish/cilok"],["Cimpa","dish/cimpa"],["Corn fritter","dish/corn-fritter"],["Coto makassar","dish/coto-makassar"],["Dadar gulung","dish/dadar-gulung"],["Dadiah","dish/dadiah"],["Dengke mas naniura","dish/dengke-mas-naniura"],["Empal gentong","dish/empal-gentong"],["Emping","dish/emping"],["Es buah","dish/es-buah"],["Es doger","dish/es-doger"],["Es goyobod","dish/es-goyobod"],["Gado-gado","dish/gado-gado"],["Geplak","dish/geplak"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/d/da/Gulai_ayam.JPG/500px-Gulai_ayam.JPG","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Midori","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gulai_ayam.JPG"}},"dish/lohikeitto":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/lohikeitto","n":"Lohikeitto","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Finnish cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Salmon chowder is a common dish in Finland and other Nordic countries. It consists of salmon fillets, boiled potatoes, carrots and leeks. The dish is served hot, and typically seasoned with fresh dill, allspice, salt and black pepper. The soup is made with cream or whole milk, making it a chowder with a creamy flavour and texture."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-lohikeitto"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lohikeitto"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Finnish cuisine","cuisine/finnish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":6,"links":[["Leek","ingredient/leek"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Salmon","ingredient/salmon"],["Salmon as food","ingredient/salmon-as-food"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]]},{"h":"More from Finnish cuisine","links":[["Blodpalt","dish/blodpalt"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Egg butter","dish/egg-butter"],["Glögg","dish/glogg"],["Joulutorttu","dish/joulutorttu"],["Kalakukko","dish/kalakukko"],["Karelian hot pot","dish/karelian-hot-pot"],["Kesäkeitto","dish/kesakeitto"],["Kringle","dish/kringle"],["Kuivaliha","dish/kuivaliha"],["Lanttulaatikko","dish/lanttulaatikko"],["Lörtsy","dish/lortsy"],["Semla","dish/semla"],["Smörgåstårta","dish/smorgastarta"],["Strong beer","dish/strong-beer"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/b/b7/Lohikeitto.jpg/500px-Lohikeitto.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Tuijasal","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lohikeitto.jpg"}},"dish/matzah-brei":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/matzah-brei","n":"Matzah brei","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Jewish cuisine","dot":"#a99fb5","intro":[["Matzah brei, sometimes spelled matzah brie, matzoh brei, or matzo brei, is a dish of Ashkenazi Jewish origin made from matzah fried with eggs. It is commonly eaten as a breakfast food during the Jewish holiday of Passover. It can be prepared either sweet or savory."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-matzah-brei"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matzah_brei"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Jewish cuisine","cuisine/jewish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":3,"links":[["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Matzah","ingredient/matzah"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Tori Avey: Jewish Recipes","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"toriavey.com","note":"Tori Avey's Jewish recipe index, often paired with notes on each dish's history.","url":"https://toriavey.com/jewish-recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"},{"name":"The Nosher (My Jewish Learning)","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"myjewishlearning.com","note":"Recipe section of the nonprofit My Jewish Learning, covering Ashkenazi, Sephardi and Mizrahi home cooking and holiday food.","url":"https://www.myjewishlearning.com/recipes/","suggested":true,"kind":"site"}]},{"h":"More from Jewish cuisine","links":[["Appetizing store","dish/appetizing-store"],["Bagel","dish/bagel"],["Brisket (Jewish dish)","dish/brisket-jewish-dish"],["Cebularz","dish/cebularz"],["Challah","dish/challah"],["Forshmak","dish/forshmak"],["Gefilte fish","dish/gefilte-fish"],["Gondi (food)","dish/gondi-food"],["Gribenes","dish/gribenes"],["Hamantash","dish/hamantash"],["Holishkes","dish/holishkes"],["Jewish deli","dish/jewish-deli"],["Lekach","dish/lekach"],["Mafrum","dish/mafrum"],["Miodownik","dish/miodownik"],["Pizza dolce di Beridde","dish/pizza-dolce-di-beridde"],["Reuben sandwich","dish/reuben-sandwich"],["Shirin polo","dish/shirin-polo"],["Sorrel soup","dish/sorrel-soup"],["Teiglach","dish/teiglach"],["Tsvikli","dish/tsvikli"],["Tzimmes","dish/tzimmes"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/2/2c/Matzah_brei_serving.jpg/500px-Matzah_brei_serving.jpg","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Yoninah","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Matzah_brei_serving.jpg"}},"ingredient/beef-tenderloin":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/beef-tenderloin","n":"Beef tenderloin","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 1 dish across 1 cuisine","intro":[["A beef tenderloin (US English), known as an eye fillet in Australasia and fillet in the United Kingdom and South Africa, is cut from the loin of beef."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-beef-tenderloin"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beef_tenderloin"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":1,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Selat solo","dish/selat-solo"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Green bean","ingredient/green-bean"],["Lettuce","ingredient/lettuce"],["Mayonnaise","ingredient/mayonnaise"],["Onion","ingredient/onion"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Soy sauce","ingredient/soy-sauce"],["Sweet soy sauce","ingredient/sweet-soy-sauce"],["Vinegar","ingredient/vinegar"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/b/b8/BeefCutTenderloin.png/500px-BeefCutTenderloin.png","w":500,"h":295,"by":"JoeSmack at English Wikipedia","lic":"Public domain","licUrl":"","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BeefCutTenderloin.png"}},"dish/shaking-beef":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/shaking-beef","n":"Shaking beef","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Vietnamese cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Shaking beef or bo luc lac is a Vietnamese dish that consists of beef that has been cut into small cubes, marinated with fish sauce, soy sauce and oyster sauce, then sauteed in a wok with red onion and bell pepper before being served on a bed of fresh lettuce or watercress with slices of tomato and cucumber. Prior to French colonization, beef was considered a luxury ingredient in Vietnam, as cows were rarely slaughtered for food and were kept as working animals to be used for labor instead. This dish was, therefore, mostly reserved for formal events, such as wedding banquets and anniversaries, although now it has become a common food."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-shaking-beef"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shaking_beef"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Vietnamese cuisine","cuisine/vietnamese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":11,"links":[["Beef","ingredient/beef"],["Bell pepper","ingredient/bell-pepper"],["Cucumber","ingredient/cucumber"],["Fish sauce","ingredient/fish-sauce"],["Lettuce","ingredient/lettuce"],["Oyster sauce","ingredient/oy